00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:44.830
Now, I think the best place
to start our journey is

00:04:44.830 --> 00:04:46.950
to explore a concept often present

00:04:46.950 --> 00:04:49.420
in application development, events.

00:04:49.420 --> 00:04:50.810
Let's get started.

00:04:50.810 --> 00:04:54.830
An event, in code, is a
defined action or occurrence.

00:04:54.830 --> 00:04:57.170
Now, these events are
typically exposed to us,

00:04:57.170 --> 00:04:59.700
software developers, so
that we can write code

00:04:59.700 --> 00:05:01.240
that runs specifically when

00:05:01.240 --> 00:05:04.020
one of these actions
or occurrences happen.

00:05:04.020 --> 00:05:06.000
The first type of event
that I'd like to talk about

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:08.920
is one that is caused by the
user of your application.

00:05:08.920 --> 00:05:11.810
Let's think about a button on a webpage.

00:05:11.810 --> 00:05:14.140
This button will trigger an event

00:05:14.140 --> 00:05:15.870
when it is clicked by an user.

00:05:15.870 --> 00:05:18.750
You can optionally write
some code that will run,

00:05:18.750 --> 00:05:21.530
and only run when that event occurs.

00:05:21.530 --> 00:05:25.070
You do this by adding or
registering what is known as

00:05:25.070 --> 00:05:26.820
an event listener.

00:05:26.820 --> 00:05:30.063
You write code, also
referred to as a handler,

00:05:30.063 --> 00:05:32.850
used to handle the specific event.

00:05:32.850 --> 00:05:34.920
In this case, click,

00:05:34.920 --> 00:05:38.030
this code will only run
when that event happens.

00:05:38.030 --> 00:05:40.330
This is called, event-driven programming.

00:05:40.330 --> 00:05:42.730
In this example, it's used to empower you

00:05:42.730 --> 00:05:45.430
to not have to write code
that continually checks

00:05:45.430 --> 00:05:47.270
to see if the button is being pressed.

00:05:47.270 --> 00:05:49.090
Just like the kids in the car,

00:05:49.090 --> 00:05:52.220
it isn't very efficient for
you to write code asking,

00:05:52.220 --> 00:05:54.120
hey, button, are you clicked yet?

00:05:54.120 --> 00:05:55.160
Are you clicked yet?

00:05:55.160 --> 00:05:57.180
Now, how 'bout now, are you clicked?

00:05:57.180 --> 00:05:59.260
The click event is just one of many events

00:05:59.260 --> 00:06:01.630
that webpages allow for listening to.

00:06:01.630 --> 00:06:02.520
Check the teacher's notes

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:04.940
for more browser-based event examples.

00:06:04.940 --> 00:06:07.730
Similar in concept to
a user-generated event,

00:06:07.730 --> 00:06:10.210
is what is known as a lifecycle event.

00:06:10.210 --> 00:06:12.710
Lifecycle events are
typically exposed to us

00:06:12.710 --> 00:06:15.600
via application libraries or frameworks.

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:17.880
You can think of application
libraries and frameworks

00:06:17.880 --> 00:06:21.910
as tools that help us rapidly
develop our applications.

00:06:21.910 --> 00:06:25.260
These libraries do this by
providing repeatable ways

00:06:25.260 --> 00:06:27.550
for us to write our applications.

00:06:27.550 --> 00:06:30.440
For example, these libraries
allow us developers

00:06:30.440 --> 00:06:32.990
to customize various
parts of the application,

00:06:32.990 --> 00:06:37.120
at specific times, in specific contexts.

00:06:37.120 --> 00:06:41.110
They allow us to hook into
specific lifecycle events.

00:06:41.110 --> 00:06:42.610
For instance, when you're building

00:06:42.610 --> 00:06:45.530
a web application these days,
you're almost certainly using

00:06:45.530 --> 00:06:48.160
a front-end application library.

00:06:48.160 --> 00:06:50.580
One of the main benefits
that these libraries give you

00:06:50.580 --> 00:06:54.940
is the ability to write code
for only your specific needs.

00:06:54.940 --> 00:06:56.610
A great example is you can write code

00:06:56.610 --> 00:06:59.120
once your component has
been loaded on the screen.

00:06:59.120 --> 00:07:01.160
This load is a lifecycle event.

00:07:01.160 --> 00:07:04.840
Usually this looks something
like, has been loaded.

00:07:04.840 --> 00:07:06.970
What's happening here is what is known as,

00:07:06.970 --> 00:07:09.040
inversion of control.

00:07:09.040 --> 00:07:12.110
Now, this is because the
control is being handed to you,

00:07:12.110 --> 00:07:13.970
the developer, from the application,

00:07:13.970 --> 00:07:15.930
at exactly the right time.

00:07:15.930 --> 00:07:19.530
The library does its magic,
it prepares your component.

00:07:19.530 --> 00:07:21.740
It then renders it out to the screen,

00:07:21.740 --> 00:07:23.850
and when it's loaded on the page,

00:07:23.850 --> 00:07:26.500
it calls the code that you wired up

00:07:26.500 --> 00:07:28.960
to your has-been-loaded hook,

00:07:28.960 --> 00:07:31.240
and that abstraction is super nice.

00:07:31.240 --> 00:07:33.010
You don't call the function yourself.

00:07:33.010 --> 00:07:36.293
The library actually calls
your function when it's ready.

00:07:38.060 --> 00:07:41.610
This inversion of control pattern
is also often referred to,

00:07:41.610 --> 00:07:43.870
as the Hollywood principle.

00:07:43.870 --> 00:07:45.800
In Hollywood, casting directors

00:07:45.800 --> 00:07:47.720
usually tell the person auditioning,

00:07:47.720 --> 00:07:49.603
don't call us, we'll call you.

00:07:49.603 --> 00:07:51.730
Now, this is because if they didn't,

00:07:51.730 --> 00:07:54.500
the actor would just keep calling them.

00:07:54.500 --> 00:07:56.720
Yeah, I was just in there auditioning.

00:07:56.720 --> 00:07:59.350
I pretty sure I got the
part, I did a really good.

00:07:59.350 --> 00:08:01.300
Weird, they hung up, I
guess I'll call back.

00:08:01.300 --> 00:08:03.220
No, I was the older guy.

00:08:03.220 --> 00:08:04.800
I was just wondering when should I come in

00:08:04.800 --> 00:08:08.270
for my wardrobe fitting, hello?

00:08:08.270 --> 00:08:10.700
Weird they disconnected, I
guess I'll try that again.

00:08:10.700 --> 00:08:12.460
Again, like my kids,

00:08:12.460 --> 00:08:15.680
these actors are totally going
to overload these directors

00:08:15.680 --> 00:08:16.930
with their needy questions.

00:08:16.930 --> 00:08:18.950
If they were allowed to call the director

00:08:18.950 --> 00:08:21.740
and check for their approval,
they'd do it all the time.

00:08:21.740 --> 00:08:23.630
That Hollywood-principle abstraction,

00:08:23.630 --> 00:08:25.470
allows the actor to not need

00:08:25.470 --> 00:08:27.440
to understand the entire process

00:08:27.440 --> 00:08:29.490
that Hollywood directors
are going through.

00:08:29.490 --> 00:08:32.911
The directors do whatever
it is that directors do.

00:08:32.911 --> 00:08:34.750
I think it involves tall
chairs, and megaphones,

00:08:34.750 --> 00:08:37.390
and snacks, definitely snacks.

00:08:37.390 --> 00:08:41.230
The point is, only when the
directors make their decision,

00:08:41.230 --> 00:08:43.320
do they call to inform the actor.

00:08:43.320 --> 00:08:46.340
These patterns of event
handlers and lifecycle hooks

00:08:46.340 --> 00:08:49.100
have really proven useful
in helping developers

00:08:49.100 --> 00:08:51.730
to build extensible applications.

00:08:51.730 --> 00:08:52.840
The good news is,

00:08:52.840 --> 00:08:56.130
that this powerful abstraction
can also span across

00:08:56.130 --> 00:08:59.410
entirely different
applications over the internet,

00:08:59.410 --> 00:09:04.090
by using what is known
as, ta-da, a webhook.

00:09:04.090 --> 00:09:06.130
Now, that we've got a
light understanding of

