00:24:05.800 --> 00:24:09.800
POST, and in the action, you'd
put the name of the script.

00:24:09.800 --> 00:24:13.310
It's important to remember,
that the web is stateless.

00:24:13.310 --> 00:24:15.600
Once this request to generate the page

00:24:15.600 --> 00:24:19.580
that the form lives on,
the web server job is done.

00:24:19.580 --> 00:24:22.520
The GET request is over.

00:24:22.520 --> 00:24:25.010
Your user would fill out the HTML form

00:24:25.010 --> 00:24:28.960
with the named fields and then
they'd click a submit button.

00:24:28.960 --> 00:24:30.730
What the browser does
for you in this case,

00:24:30.730 --> 00:24:33.340
is it takes all the values
that were entered in the form,

00:24:33.340 --> 00:24:36.480
and it makes a brand new HTTP request

00:24:36.480 --> 00:24:40.670
to the URL that you specified
in the action attribute.

00:24:40.670 --> 00:24:42.780
That POST request contains the data

00:24:42.780 --> 00:24:45.473
that the user entered in an encoded way.

00:24:46.400 --> 00:24:47.790
As a developer,

00:24:47.790 --> 00:24:50.610
you would write a request
handler on your website

00:24:50.610 --> 00:24:53.090
to capture the information
from this request,

00:24:53.090 --> 00:24:54.950
and then you'd act on it.

00:24:54.950 --> 00:24:58.933
Again, your browser is making
a request to your server.

00:24:59.820 --> 00:25:01.440
Way back in 2007,

00:25:01.440 --> 00:25:03.560
a developer by the name of Jeff Lindsay,

00:25:03.560 --> 00:25:07.690
made a proposal to use an
existing idea already very present

00:25:07.690 --> 00:25:09.390
in software design patterns.

00:25:09.390 --> 00:25:13.360
He proposed to use the hook
model that we talked about,

00:25:13.360 --> 00:25:14.910
but instead of locally,

00:25:14.910 --> 00:25:17.820
he suggested to do it over the internet,

00:25:17.820 --> 00:25:20.010
and he called it, a webhook.

00:25:20.010 --> 00:25:22.510
Jeff realized that it would
be fairly straightforward

00:25:22.510 --> 00:25:25.390
for web developers who
were already very familiar

00:25:25.390 --> 00:25:29.080
with processing form data
submitted via a POST request.

00:25:29.080 --> 00:25:31.240
What this approach would
feel like to developers,

00:25:31.240 --> 00:25:33.830
is just another form submission.

00:25:33.830 --> 00:25:36.770
In this case, instead of the
user submitting the form,

00:25:36.770 --> 00:25:39.290
a third party could submit the data.

00:25:39.290 --> 00:25:41.080
The handler could be written the same way

00:25:41.080 --> 00:25:42.610
the developer already knows.

00:25:42.610 --> 00:25:45.010
The information is pushed to the handler,

00:25:45.010 --> 00:25:46.730
in that same familiar way,

00:25:46.730 --> 00:25:49.113
to the developer and everyone wins.

00:25:50.020 --> 00:25:51.650
In order for this to work,

00:25:51.650 --> 00:25:54.750
the third-party application
needs to be informed

00:25:54.750 --> 00:25:58.890
from the developer where they
should post their information.

00:25:58.890 --> 00:26:01.670
You know, the URL where
that handler lives.

00:26:01.670 --> 00:26:04.750
And then, when the
third-party event happens,

00:26:04.750 --> 00:26:08.950
it posts the information
about the event to that URL.

00:26:08.950 --> 00:26:09.783
(icon exploding)

00:26:09.783 --> 00:26:11.340
That app is definitely embracing

00:26:11.340 --> 00:26:12.800
the Hollywood principle, right?

00:26:12.800 --> 00:26:15.053
Don't call us, we'll call you.

00:26:16.230 --> 00:26:17.270
It took off,

00:26:17.270 --> 00:26:20.150
for all the reasons that
event-based programming wins.

00:26:20.150 --> 00:26:22.810
It's, of course, way
more efficient to receive

00:26:22.810 --> 00:26:25.760
a POST request, than
continuously polling an API,

00:26:25.760 --> 00:26:27.090
checking for a change.

00:26:27.090 --> 00:26:29.150
Instead of, are we there
yet, are we there yet?

00:26:29.150 --> 00:26:33.920
Via an API, the POST
request sends, we're here!

00:26:33.920 --> 00:26:38.290
That was 2007, webhooks
are now everywhere.

00:26:38.290 --> 00:26:40.960
I'm guessing what brought
you to this course,

00:26:40.960 --> 00:26:43.310
was that you encountered one in the wild,

00:26:43.310 --> 00:26:45.310
and you weren't exactly
sure how to use it.

00:26:45.310 --> 00:26:47.770
No worries, this happens to everyone

00:26:47.770 --> 00:26:49.770
the first time they run across one.

00:26:49.770 --> 00:26:52.960
It's a bit challenging
to understand at first,

00:26:52.960 --> 00:26:56.060
the web has changed a lot since 2007,

00:26:56.060 --> 00:27:00.130
but the webhook concept here,
is still pretty much the same.

00:27:00.130 --> 00:27:02.020
Our apps are now built differently.

00:27:02.020 --> 00:27:04.880
Like for instance, we
may be using services,

00:27:04.880 --> 00:27:06.100
and instead of form post,

00:27:06.100 --> 00:27:09.210
we build our own APIs
for our clients to use.

00:27:09.210 --> 00:27:11.570
The client post information to our API,

00:27:11.570 --> 00:27:13.570
usually not using a form.

00:27:13.570 --> 00:27:16.630
We developers use
web-based APIs all the time

00:27:16.630 --> 00:27:18.090
to build our applications.

00:27:18.090 --> 00:27:19.390
You should view webhooks

00:27:19.390 --> 00:27:21.830
as an extension of that type of work.

00:27:21.830 --> 00:27:25.380
This is why sometimes you'll
hear webhooks referred to as,

00:27:25.380 --> 00:27:27.970
a reverse API.

00:27:27.970 --> 00:27:31.410
It calls you, instead of you calling it.

00:27:31.410 --> 00:27:32.590
When you use an API,

00:27:32.590 --> 00:27:35.840
you need to understand the
parameters that it expects,

00:27:35.840 --> 00:27:38.120
and the reverse is true for webhooks.

00:27:38.120 --> 00:27:41.090
You need to know what
information is being sent to you,

00:27:41.090 --> 00:27:42.450
and in what format.

00:27:42.450 --> 00:27:44.950
There's no formal specification,

00:27:44.950 --> 00:27:47.850
and any data can be sent to your handler.

00:27:47.850 --> 00:27:50.660
Now these days, our web
handlers most likely look

00:27:50.660 --> 00:27:53.420
a wee-bit different than they did in 2007.

00:27:53.420 --> 00:27:55.000
Technology has evolved,

00:27:55.000 --> 00:27:58.440
some handlers still accept
traditional, form-posted data,

00:27:58.440 --> 00:28:01.470
and some of our handlers accept
JSON, posted in the body,

00:28:01.470 --> 00:28:04.140
for client side, the APIs
that we're writing, right?

00:28:04.140 --> 00:28:06.590
See the teacher's notes for more on this.

00:28:06.590 --> 00:28:08.790
You'll find a variety in what

00:28:08.790 --> 00:28:11.040
webhooks post to your handlers as well.

00:28:11.040 --> 00:28:13.720
As a developer, writing one
of these webhook handlers,

00:28:13.720 --> 00:28:15.640
it's pretty important that you understand

00:28:15.640 --> 00:28:18.980
how to capture and parse
that incoming data.

