this magic through webhooks.

01:36:57.860 --> 01:37:01.230
We talked a while back
about lifecycle hooks.

01:37:01.230 --> 01:37:03.570
This is the concept where
the flow of the application

01:37:03.570 --> 01:37:07.110
allows you to hook into it and
the control is passed to you.

01:37:07.110 --> 01:37:08.790
You're responsible to respond

01:37:08.790 --> 01:37:11.290
with what the application should do next.

01:37:11.290 --> 01:37:14.800
That's the main concept for
what happens with incoming input

01:37:14.800 --> 01:37:18.200
to your Twilio number, a call
or a text message, right?

01:37:18.200 --> 01:37:19.600
The control of the application

01:37:19.600 --> 01:37:22.210
is passed to you via a webhook.

01:37:22.210 --> 01:37:24.540
There are a ton of other
webhooks that allow you

01:37:24.540 --> 01:37:27.060
to control what happens
at different times,

01:37:27.060 --> 01:37:28.700
when different events occur,

01:37:28.700 --> 01:37:32.420
continually passing control
back to your webhook handlers.

01:37:32.420 --> 01:37:36.070
Because, your handler is
any public accessible URL,

01:37:36.070 --> 01:37:38.130
your code can be in any
programming language.

01:37:38.130 --> 01:37:39.920
It just needs to respond

01:37:39.920 --> 01:37:43.080
with what you want Twilio to do next.

01:37:43.080 --> 01:37:45.750
Twilio's basically a webhooks playground.

01:37:45.750 --> 01:37:48.420
There are an impressive
amount of them to play with.

